<p>
  The report's key caveat on RSI: <em>"purely digital researchers are still bounded by having to run larger and larger experiments and wait for their outcomes."</em> The real world runs at real-time speed. Even an ASI can't run a genomics trial in minutes — but it could run thousands in parallel. The bottleneck isn't intelligence; it's the physical world's clock speed.
</p>

<div class="callout callout-warning">
  <strong>Gödel's limit on RSI:</strong> Self-modifying systems provably cannot verify their own modifications are improvements (Gödel incompleteness, Halting Problem). Schmidhuber's Gödel machines offer a theoretically sound framework for provably safe self-modification — but remain impractical. Christiano et al.'s iterated amplification is a more pragmatic alternative.
</div>
</section>

<!-- ─── §05 Bottlenecks ─── -->
<section id="s-friction" data-code="friction">
<div class="section-no">05 — Bottlenecks &amp; Frictions</div>
<h2>What Could Slow the Transition?</h2>
<p>
  The report is admirably honest: none of the four pathways are guaranteed. Seven major bottlenecks could substantially slow — and in some cases, halt — progress from AGI to ASI. For each, the report identifies both the friction and the potential counter. The significance of each is explicitly called an <em>open research question</em>.
</p>

<div class="friction-grid" id="friction-grid">

  <div class="friction-card">
    <h4>🧱 The Data Wall</h4>
    <p>Current model growth outpaces novel text production. Naive self-generated training data causes "performance plateau and degeneration" (Shumailov et al., 2024). Counter: test-time search → distillation (AlphaZero-style), simulation, agentic interaction data.</p>
    <div class="gauge-track"><div class="gauge-fill" style="background:rgba(245,158,11,.7);width:0%" data-w="78"></div></div>
    <div class="gauge-labels"><span>low friction</span><span>high friction</span></div>
  </div>

  <div class="friction-card">
    <h4>⚡ Energy &amp; Physical Resources</h4>
    <p>Landauer's principle bounds computation energy. Sustaining 10× annual compute growth requires exponentially growing power. Data center energy demand may hit physical grid limits before ASI. Counter: neuromorphic/analog computing, algorithmic efficiency.</p>
    <div class="gauge-track"><div class="gauge-fill" style="background:rgba(245,158,11,.7);width:0%" data-w="65"></div></div>
    <div class="gauge-labels"><span>low friction</span><span>high friction</span></div>
  </div>

  <div class="friction-card">
    <h4>💰 Economics of Scale</h4>
    <p>Co-scaling compute, data, and energy is enormously expensive. GPT-4 training cost ~$100M; each 10× step is another ~$1B+. Whether economic incentives can sustain this is uncertain — and concentrated investment creates geopolitical risks.</p>
    <div class="gauge-track"><div class="gauge-fill" style="background:rgba(139,92,246,.7);width:0%" data-w="55"></div></div>
    <div class="gauge-labels"><span>low friction</span><span>high friction</span></div>
  </div>

  <div class="friction-card">
    <h4>🧠 Abstraction Barrier</h4>
    <p>Biological intelligence built on embodied experience; AI trained on human text may learn coarse abstractions insufficient for true general reasoning. Whether internet-scale pretraining overcomes this is unresolved.</p>
    <div class="gauge-track"><div class="gauge-fill" style="background:rgba(6,182,212,.7);width:0%" data-w="50"></div></div>
    <div class="gauge-labels"><span>low friction</span><span>high friction</span></div>
  </div>

  <div class="friction-card">
    <h4>🔬 Real-Time Experimental Bottleneck</h4>
    <p>Even recursive self-improvement must wait for experiments — drug trials, hardware fabrication, climate models. The physical world's clock speed cannot be accelerated by intelligence alone. Counter: better simulation, digital twins, in-silico experiments.</p>
    <div class="gauge-track"><div class="gauge-fill" style="background:rgba(6,182,212,.7);width:0%" data-w="60"></div></div>
    <div class="gauge-labels"><span>low friction</span><span>high friction</span></div>
  </div>

  <div class="friction-card">
    <h4>⚖️ Governance &amp; Regulation</h4>
    <p>Rogue-actor risks, accidents, or sociocultural backlash could trigger deliberate regulatory caps on AI capability. Counter: international race dynamics and economic pressures may override slow-down signals, especially without global coordination.</p>
    <div class="gauge-track"><div class="gauge-fill" style="background:rgba(239,68,68,.7);width:0%" data-w="45"></div></div>
    <div class="gauge-labels"><span>low friction</span><span>high friction</span></div>
  </div>

</div>

<div class="callout callout-note">
  <strong>The report's key claim:</strong> Frictions are likely to slow but not halt progress — but the impact of each is a genuinely open research question. This report is unusual in naming what it doesn't know.
</div>
</section>

<!-- ─── §06 AIXI Upper Bound ─── -->
<section id="s-aixi" data-code="aixi">
<div class="section-no">06 — The AIXI Upper Bound</div>
<h2>What Formal Theory Says About Intelligence Limits</h2>
<p>
  What's the theoretical ceiling of machine intelligence? The report uses the AIXI framework (Hutter, 2005) as the best-understood formal upper bound. AIXI is the agent that maximizes expected cumulative reward averaged over <em>all computable environments</em> weighted by their inverse Kolmogorov complexity.
</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">AIXI optimality</div>
  <span class="math-var">AIXI</span> = argmax<sub>policy π</sub> 𝔼<sub>μ ~ Universal Prior</sub>[Σ<sub>t</sub> reward(a<sub>t</sub>, o<sub>t</sub>)]<br>
  <br>
  <span class="math-comment">// Prior: P(μ) ∝ 2^(-K(μ)) — simpler environments are exponentially more likely</span><br>
  <span class="math-comment">// Optimal in sense: no agent achieves higher expected reward under this prior</span><br>
  <span class="math-comment">// Incomputable — but approximable from below with more compute</span>
</div>

<p>
  Three problems AIXI solves simultaneously — and which today's models approximate imperfectly:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Acting under uncertainty:</strong> Bayesian posterior over all computable environments; updates as observations arrive. Today's LLMs do implicit Bayesian reasoning but without explicit world model tracking.</li>
  <li><strong>Credit assignment:</strong> General RL over arbitrary reward functions. Today's RLHF/RLAIF is a narrow instance — fixed reward functions, short horizons.</li>
  <li><strong>Exploration vs. exploitation:</strong> Automatically resolved in AIXI — exploratory actions become high-reward when they reduce uncertainty about the reward function. Today's systems need explicit exploration bonuses.</li>
</ul>

<p>
  The report's crucial theoretical argument: training a massive transformer to minimize log-loss over internet-scale data is a <em>resource-bounded approximation of universal compression</em> — and this scales toward AIXI. This gives non-trivial theoretical support for the pretraining paradigm reaching ASI territory, without requiring a fundamental breakthrough.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">aixi_hierarchy.py — intelligence spectrum from current systems to theoretical upper bound</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">hover to explore</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="aixi-canvas" style="width:100%;height:220px;display:block"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  AIXI also provides the key message about what ASI <em>cannot</em> do: it's bounded by computability (Turing/Gödel), physics (speed of light, Landauer's principle, Bekenstein bound), NP-hardness for combinatorial problems, and the real-time clock speed of the physical world. ASI will be extraordinary — but not omniscient or omnipotent.
</p>

<div class="callout callout-insight">
  <strong>Bottom line:</strong> The transition from AGI to ASI is unlikely to be a single dramatic moment — it's more likely a series of compounding advances across the four pathways, each amplifying the others, constrained by real-world frictions. Preparing for this requires, as the paper concludes, "a massively interdisciplinary endeavour of global scope and interest."
</div>
</section>

